{
  "schema_version": "0.1.0",
  "fixture_id": "bags-flip-the-fare",
  "title": "New York to Madrid: every headline price is wrong once two bags are counted, and the cheapest fare finishes last",
  "pins_down": "Baggage is a reduction over TICKET TOPOLOGY, not a lookup on a fare name: fees index from beyond the included allowance, tiers reset per passenger, and a bag crossing two ticketing boundaries is paid for twice under two different carriers' schedules.",
  "as_of": "2026-03-01",
  "notes": [
    "The brief said 'basic economy plus two bags often beats the main cabin fare it undercut.' The arithmetic holds and this fixture demonstrates it - but the reasoning behind it in the brief does not. The brief assumed many transatlantic Basic/Light fares still include a checked bag. For 2026 NYC-Europe that is false: of thirteen carriers in scope, exactly one bundles a checked bag into the fare it calls Basic. The rest are bag-free at their cheapest tier. Anything built on pre-2024 intuition here will be wrong.",
    "That one exception is TAP, and it is in this fixture on purpose: TAP Basic DOES include a 23kg bag, and only its cheaper 'Discount' tier has none. This is the fixture that proves the scorer must key baggage off a structured entitlements block and never off the brand string or a normalised 'is_basic' flag. Two carriers, both saying 'Basic', opposite allowances.",
    "The brief also said United's transatlantic Basic 'notoriously bars the overhead bin'. True at launch, not the 2026 rule - transatlantic Basic Economy carries a full-size carry-on. What survives is real but different: Basic boards last, so bin space is often gone. That is a comfort line about boarding group, not a baggage exclusion, and conflating them would charge a fee that does not exist.",
    "American's first-bag fee is keyed to TICKET ISSUE DATE, not travel date - $75 for tickets issued before 2026-05-17, $85 on or after 2026-05-18. Both AA options here carry an issue_date for exactly that reason. A fixture with only a travel date cannot resolve the right fee, and the scorer would silently pick one.",
    "The self-transfer option is the trap the whole schema is shaped around. Two separately issued tickets means two fee assessments, two carriers' tier schedules, no through-checked bags and no protected connection. Its intra-Europe leg's first-bag fee is HIGHER than the transatlantic carrier's, which is the opposite of what anyone expects.",
    "The intra-Europe ticket is filed in EUR with a pinned FX rate. The USD figure is derived, never stored - otherwise the conversion path is baked in and never executes under test.",
    "The expected order was corrected once the scorer ran. Hand arithmetic had put TAP ahead of American's Basic on cash-with-bags ($563 against $583) and that much is right - but TAP connects, so it is three hours longer door to door with a 30-inch seat, and at any hourly value above about $7 that costs more than the $20 fare gap. Counting exactly this is the product; the hand arithmetic was the thing that was wrong. What the fixture set out to prove still holds: Basic was $57 cheaper on the ticket and finishes behind Main Cabin, and the cheapest headline on the page finishes last by a wide margin."
  ],
